foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday November 30th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  over the last couple of years Insurance  firms have slowly been Gathering a  clearer picture of what cyber attacks  cost their clients  a few major incidents like the not petia  virus that invaded corporate software in  2017 or the attack on the colonial  pipeline last year have shown just how  big the Fallout from cyber attacks can  be  but there still hasn't been a truly  catastrophic Cyber attack  so how is the Cyber insurance industry  preparing for one  joining us to discuss that is wsj pro  cyber security reporter James Rundle hi  James thanks for joining us Hey Zoe  thanks for having me so James since 2020  we've seen this big pickup in the number  of claims for cyber attacks how have  insurance companies been adjusting their  policies to deal with this yeah  particularly from 2020 there was a huge  uptick in claims basically from cyber  attacks of all Stripes whether that's  ransomware or other forms and  it really knocked the insurance industry  back a little bit I think a lot of  insurers got into cyber thinking it was  a really lucrative source of Revenue but  then as the claims started to pile up  they started to look at it and realize  that actually we're not being as  disciplined without under icing as maybe  we should be so in reaction to that  premiums went up across the board by  significant amounts and insurers already  started asking more of the people who  wanted to be policyholders they wanted  to know exactly what kind of cyber  defenses they had in place and what  level of protections they were putting  to secure their systems in and they were  refusing cover for people who didn't  have it okay so higher standards for the  people who are purchasing these cyber  insurance policies but how do insurers  determine you know what the payout  should be when there is a Cyber attack  well it depends on the level of coverage  you buy I mean cyber Insurance generally  covers a number of areas so you'll have  your initial instant response so you  might have to hire a cyber security  company to come in and find out what  happens you have the legal counsel you  need to retain in order to meet any  regulatory obligations you have  communications for crisis scenarios and  everything else there is a whole battery  of costs that are involved in that  depending on the size of the  organization of course those costs will  increase or decrease accordingly so even  with the growing number of policies out  there and with the growing number of  payouts that insurers have had to make  there's still this concern that they're  not prepared for a catastrophic Cyber  attack can you I guess start by sort of  telling us what a catastrophic Cyber  attack would actually mean how does that  compare to other cyber attacks because  I'm sure some companies would feel  pretty catastrophic when they've been  hit absolutely and I think it's  important to stress that we don't  actually know because we haven't had one  yet when we talk about catastrophic  hacks we're not talking about  a single company being taken down or a  Cyber attack that causes you know  several million dollars in damage which  is you know pretty catastrophic for  those involved but what we're talking  about here is something that really  affects the economy or affects the  stability of society so we're the  collapse of the financial system for  instance or exchanges being taken down  Clearing Houses things are vital to the  running of the country that if they were  to stop working the country would  effectively stop working as well which  is why it's one of those things that  people hope doesn't happen but they're  worried will eventually I mean can you  give us an example of what one of those  companies is because we have seen  Fallout from you know a Cyber attack on  one company affect other companies  we have one of the scenarios that the  people I speak to my article are working  on is what happens if one of the major  Cloud providers gets taken down so  you're talking like an Amazon here or a  Google or a Microsoft or someone like  that because these people don't just  power one company in a specific industry  where there would be some Downstream  effects they power huge amounts of  companies across huge numbers of  critical infrastructure sectors whether  that's Finance retail Logistics  Healthcare that if they were to go down  it would have severe repercussions  across a range of areas so given that we  haven't had one of these catastrophic  cyber attacks how do insurers make  models of what they might be dealing  with in that scenario well that's the  big question it's what they're  struggling with right now the problem is  cyber security as an area to ensure is  something called the contagion effect  which what we've been alluding to where  the impact of an attack on one company  can have far-reaching consequences among  many many others and it takes a  incredible degree of complex mathematics  to be able to even attempt to model how  that would happen and the fact of the  matter is you just don't know you don't  know how one company being taken down  will affect another which in turn will  affect another which in turn will affect  another it's extraordinarily difficult  to try and figure out exactly what would  happen if the bank of England would be  taken down or the London Stock Exchange  or the New York Stock Exchange  simultaneously or something like that  much harder is that to model than say  like a category five hurricane where you  might have a lot of Fallout and one  thing could affect another could take  out homes and power grids I mean they do  have some precedent right well that's  the thing we have had category five  hurricanes before and we've had natural  disasters before on Epic scales that we  have the data where we can look at that  and say okay well historically this is  what happened and we can use that to  extrapolate what would happen now and  our more connected society and  everything else the issue with cyber  security is that we simply don't have  that information we only have small  relatively small scale events that we  can use to guess what would happen on a  larger scale but the truth is we just  don't know and we were talking about  this ripple effect where you know one  company faces a Cyber attack and it  affects all these other Downstream  companies but are insurers thinking  about what the impact on them might be I  mean how do they prepare for the  potential risk to their business if this  happens for sure that's the big  existential question really is if one of  these were to happen would it be so  large as to actually take down the  carriers themselves would they not be  able to cover their obligations and the  claims in this instance or the  reinsurers who ensure the insurers you  know what would they be on the hook for  as well and so we started to see some  moves towards an element of risk  management in this area Lloyds of London  which is not an insurer but is a  significant Marketplace for insurers  recently directed its syndicates to  adopt language that would exclude  state-backed catastrophic attacks from  their insurance policies because as we  said the risk is so great they simply  don't know how the industry would cope  it would be a systemic event not just  for society but for the insurance  industry as well we're also starting to  see I mean from my reporting people were  telling me that  some insurers are excluding specific  Cloud providers from their coverage  saying you know something goes wrong  we'll only cover up to a certain amount  of Damages or we will not cover if this  happens so they are starting to look at  this and think okay well we need to put  some limits in place we need to put some  exclusions in place to make sure that  we're protected as well and that we can  provide the services that we're offering  all right that's our reporter James  Rundle James thanks so much for  explaining this for us thanks sir and  that's it for today's Tech news briefing  for more Tech stories check out our  website wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The  Wall Street Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

